<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Intentionally empty.

	     Do not fill this in by hand. Entitlements must be a subset of what the
	     provisioning profile grants, and that set is different for a free
	     Apple ID, a paid team, and an enterprise cert. A signer derives them
	     from the profile, which is always right; this file exists only so a
	     signer that demands a path has one, and an empty file can never be
	     worse than a hand-written one.

	     Generate the real file from your own profile with
	     tools/make_sideload_entitlements.py (see native/INSTALL.md section 2b).
	     It reads the .mobileprovision, rewrites application-identifier and the
	     keychain group to the IPA's actual bundle id, and reports expiry,
	     wildcard, enterprise and missing-UDID problems.

	     What must never be in it, and why:

	     get-task-allow
	       It only permits debugging (ptrace / task_for_pid on our own task).
	       An LC_LOAD_DYLIB is resolved by dyld whether or not the app is
	       debuggable; the old claim that it is required was wrong. Meanwhile
	       it is the cheapest integrity signal a game can read about itself:
	       one SecTaskCopyValueForEntitlement call and the app knows it was
	       signed for development. A development profile still grants it, so
	       Core/Integrity.m stops answering true at runtime for builds that
	       carry it.

	     application-identifier / keychain-access-groups / team-identifier
	       These must match the certificate's Team ID and the bundle id the
	       profile actually allows. A hardcoded placeholder is worse than
	       absent: it either breaks signing or produces a mismatched identity.
	       Omitting them is not harmless either: keychain calls then return
	       -34018 and the game cannot stay logged in.

	     com.apple.security.network.client
	       A macOS App Sandbox key. iOS never consults it, so it buys nothing
	       and only adds a key the profile does not grant. Removed on purpose.

	     com.apple.private.security.no-container / platform-application
	       Private, jailbreak-class entitlements. They cannot be signed into a
	       normal sideload. They live in entitlements.trollstore.plist, which
	       is only for an install that can actually grant them. -->
</dict>
</plist>
